{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.no_normalisation_at_h_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.no_normalisation_at_h_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WritheNormalisation.lean#L103-L110","doc":"**The boundary of the construction.** At `h = 1` the two stabilisation\nconstraints are contradictory, so no monomial normalisation exists. This is the honest boundary of the construction, not an edge case to be\nwaved away. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_unique_up_to_sign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_unique_up_to_sign","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WritheNormalisation.lean#L90-L97","doc":"**Uniqueness up to sign.** Two normalisations satisfying the same two\nstabilisation constraints have equal squares, so over an integral domain they\ndiffer only by a sign. No tuning is possible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.factor_collapses","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.factor_collapses","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WritheNormalisation.lean#L82-L88","doc":"`A^e B^n` collapses to a power of `A` alone: since `B = A⁻¹`, the correction\ndepends only on `e − n`, which is why the normalisation is written\n`(1−h)^{(e−n)/2}`. Stated in the `n = e` case, where it is the bare statement\nthat the correction is trivial exactly on zero-`(e−n)` braids. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_B_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_B_sq","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WritheNormalisation.lean#L75-L80","doc":"The companion relation on `B`: it is the inverse square root of `1 − h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WritheNormalisation.writhe_factor_sq","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WritheNormalisation.lean#L65-L73","doc":"**The normalisation factor is forced.** If `A^e B^n` corrects the coefficient\nsum to a Markov-stabilisation invariant — `A * B = 1` from the positive move,\n`A = (1−h) * B` from the negative one — then `A² = 1 − h`. There is no remaining\nfreedom: the exponent `(e − n)/2` and the base `1 − h` are both derived. ","signatureChunk":0}]}